    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Second Amendment debate primarily centers on the originalist constraint that the right to keep and bear arms applies only to a militia, versus the living constitutionalist view that it guarantees an individual right. Originalists argue for a narrow, historical reading limited to collectivist purposes, while living constitutionalists advocate for a broad, evolving interpretation to protect subjective individual liberty. This tension frequently results in highly technical legal rulings that attempt to balance historical text with modern societal needs. Ultimately, the conflict reflects a deeper philosophical disagreement on whether the Constitution should be fixed or flexible over time.",
      "source": "target",
