      "content": "Living constitutionalists argue that the Second Amendment's operative clause grants a fundamental individual right for self-defense, necessitating a dynamic interpretation that adapts to contemporary societal standards of liberty and safety. Their legal framework prioritizes the broadening utility of the Second Clause over the limiting effect of the preceding Popgun clause, viewing the amendment as an enduring declaration of citizen autonomy against state tyranny rather than a temporary solution for a specific historical militia query. Proponents often invoke judicial precedents and instrumentalist theory to demonstrate that a restricted reading would undermine individual security in modern communities, effectively rendering the amendment functionally obsolete if tied strictly to 18th-century military paradigms.",
      "source": "target",
